   4: DEFECT (filed 2026-10-02).  src/cpus/cpu_ppc_instr.c, X(to_be_translated): when the instruction word is not in a host
   5: page, the translator reads it with cpu->memory_rw(..., MEM_READ, CACHE_INSTRUCTION); if that read FAILED it printed
   6: "PPC to_be_translated(): read failed: TODO" and called exit(1) -- the emulator process ended.
   7: 
   8: THE FIX (#NNN).  The read-failure arm has four exits and a fallback: (a) read-ahead returns untouched; (b) a fetch whose
   9: translation already delivered an exception (pc, its low two bits cleared, != addr) refreshes next_ic and returns; (c) a -T halt
  10: (memory.c cleared running) parks the CPU on nothing_call at the fetch's pc (its low two bits kept; the halt report names the
  11: aligned address); (d) a device declining an instruction-width read while running delivers a machine check (vector 0x200) when
  12: MSR[ME] is set, and a checkstop (text, running = 0, park) when it is clear.
  13: 
  14: WHICH FETCHES REACH IT (measured 2026-11-04).  An IR-on fetch from an unmapped address SURVIVES on the pre-fix build: the ISI
  15: is delivered by the dispatch PC lookup before the translator runs (the `isi` rows).  What reaches the arm is a fetch that
  16: translates and then fails as a PHYSICAL read: with `-T` (halt on a non-existent memory access) a real-mode fetch past the end
  17: of RAM.  Without `-T` the same fetch reads zeros and the CPU stops on the zero word (the `noT` rows).
  18: 
